---
// src/pages/projects/dice-poker.astro
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout title="Dice Poker Board — Nicholas Grzelak">
  <article class="project-page">
    <header class="project-header">
      <p class="label">M-001 · Miscellaneous</p>
      <h1>Dice Poker Board</h1>
      <p class="project-lede">
        A 4-player, poker-like game that relies on dice, wits and teamwork.
      </p>
      <p class="label project-facts">
        2026 · Autodesk Inventor · Laser cutting · DFM / DFA
      </p>
    </header>

    <!--
      Images: drop files into the /public folder of your project, e.g.
      public/projects/plotter/render-01.jpg, then reference them from the
      site root like below. Delete this placeholder block once you have
      real images.
    -->
    <figure class="project-figure">
      <img src="/projects/dicepoker/IMG_5043.jpg" alt="Freshly Engraved Dice Poker Board" />
      <!-- <img src="/projects/plotter/render-01.jpg" alt="Isometric CAD render of the plotter frame" /> -->
      <figcaption class="label">FIG. 1 — Freshly Engraved Dice Poker Board</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>Overview</h2>
    <p>
      This was a gift that someone wanted, and I thought: how hard could it be?
      This project showed me the limitations of Inventor, and of my laser cutting
       pipeline.
    </p>

    <h2>Design</h2>
    <p>
      The game was inspired by many online versions, so coming up with the design was easy.
      It was fun to use Inventor to make the houses; it required constant tweaking of parameters.
    </p>
    <p>
      CADing all the squares was tricky. Initially I thought I could make a single template and then copy that onto the 9x9 board,
      but that kind of sketch brought Inventor to a crawl. Instead I relied on smaller patterns and used mirrors where possible.
    </p>
    <p>
      I was designing for the laser and knew that it would be slow to have all the inner area engraved, so that's why most of the board is outlined.
      I liked the design choice, and it separates it from some of the other boards online. However, on the laser cutter I didn't configure the cut for vector engraving,
      and the raster engraving took... 11 hours. Yeah, that's a long time and a big mistake.
    </p>

    <h2>Results</h2>
    <p>
      Besides some of the computational issues in CAD and the laser configuration, the board came out well. There is a flaw somewhere on the board — see if you can spot it.
      After the cut I did some sanding and applied a clear coat. It came out okay, but I definitely need some future woodworking projects to get better at finishing.
    </p>

    <p class="back-link"><a href="/">← Back to index</a></p>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .project-page {
    padding: 4rem 0 1rem;
  }

  .project-header {
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
  }

  .project-header h1 {
    font-size: clamp(1.7rem, 4vw, 2.4rem);
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }

  .project-lede {
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
    max-width: 52ch;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .project-facts {
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
  }

  .project-page h2 {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    font-weight: 400;
    margin: 2.5rem 0 0.75rem;
  }

  .project-figure {
    margin: 2.5rem 0;
  }

  .project-figure figcaption {
    margin-top: 0.6rem;
  }

  .img-placeholder {
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    text-align: center;
    padding: 1rem;
  }

  .back-link {
    margin-top: 3.5rem;
  }
</style>